    <!-- Add Activity -->

 <activity>
     <title>Discovering the Cosine Rule Using String and Cardboard</title>
     <statement>
        <p>
            Work in groups of 3–4 to physically investigate the relationship between the sides and an included angle of a triangle. Through hands-on construction and measurement, you will discover and derive the Cosine Rule.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Learning Objective</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            Investigate the relationship between the sides and an included angle of a triangle and derive the Cosine Rule through direct measurement and algebraic reasoning.
        </p>

        <p>
            <alert>Materials Needed</alert>
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Manila paper or cardboard</p></li>
            <li><p>String (various lengths)</p></li>
            <li><p>Ruler (at least 30 cm)</p></li>
            <li><p>Protractor</p></li>
            <li><p>Scissors</p></li>
            <li><p>Marker pens</p></li>
            <li><p>Calculator</p></li>
            <li><p>Push pins or thumbtacks (for extension activity)</p></li>
        </ul>

        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 1: Construct a Triangle.</alert> In your group:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>Draw a large triangle ABC on the cardboard.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Label the sides as follows:
                        <ul>
                            <li><m>AB = c</m></li>
                            <li><m>BC = a</m></li>
                            <li><m>AC = b</m></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p></li>
                    <li><p>Using a ruler, measure all three side lengths as accurately as possible.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Using a protractor, measure angle <m>C</m> (the angle at vertex <m>C</m> between sides <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Record all measurements in the table below.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <table xml:id="triangle-measurements-table">
                    <title>Original Triangle Measurements</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Measurement</cell>
                            <cell>Value (cm or degrees)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>a</m> (BC)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>b</m> (AC)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Side <m>c</m> (AB)</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Angle <m>C</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Encourage students to measure multiple times and take an average for accuracy. The triangle should be drawn large (at least 15 cm on each side) to minimize measurement errors.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 2: Create a Right Triangle by Drawing a Perpendicular.</alert>
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>From vertex <m>A</m>, draw a perpendicular line to side <m>BC</m> (or the line containing <m>BC</m>).</p></li>
                    <li><p>Label the foot of the perpendicular as point <m>D</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Observe that the original triangle has now been divided into two right-angled triangles: <m>\triangle ACD</m> and <m>\triangle ABD</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Students may notice that if angle <m>C</m> is obtuse, point <m>D</m> will fall outside the original triangle (on the extension of side <m>BC</m>). This is fine and should be discussed as a valid case.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Step 3: Measure the Components of the Right Triangle.</alert>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Carefully measure the following distances on your cardboard diagram:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p><m>AD</m> (the perpendicular height)</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>DC</m> (the horizontal distance from <m>D</m> to <m>C</m>)</p></li>
                    <li><p><m>BD</m> (the horizontal distance from <m>B</m> to <m>D</m>)</p></li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    Record these measurements below:
                </p>
                <table xml:id="perpendicular-measurements-table">
                    <title>Perpendicular Components</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Measurement</cell>
                            <cell>Measured Value (cm)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Height <m>AD</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Distance <m>DC</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Distance <m>BD</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <p>
                    Now, using angle <m>C</m> from Step 1, calculate what <m>DC</m> and <m>AD</m> should be theoretically:
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>DC = b\cos C</mrow>
                    <mrow>AD = b\sin C</mrow>
                </md>
                <p>
                    <strong>Show your calculations:</strong>
                </p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>DC = b\cos C = \underline{\hspace{3cm}} \times \cos(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                    <mrow>AD = b\sin C = \underline{\hspace{3cm}} \times \sin(\underline{\hspace{1cm}}) = \underline{\hspace{2cm}}\text{ cm}</mrow>
                </md>
                <table xml:id="calculated-components-table">
                    <title>Calculated vs. Measured Components</title>
                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Component</cell>
                            <cell>Measured (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Calculated (cm)</cell>
                            <cell>Difference</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>DC</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell><m>AD</m></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        This step connects trigonometry to the geometry. Small differences between measured and calculated values are expected and should be discussed as measurement error.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </activity>
    

